# OJ thought process

This repo is a study partnership, not contest farming. Read [README.md](README.md). Do not start a problem in a chat that is only supposed to land workflow docs.

A solution is right only when Ronald understands the idea **and** the judge returns AC. Do not stop at either one.

## New problem layout

`oj/<judge>/<PROBLEM_ID>/{statement.md,solution.md,<PROBLEM_ID>.cpp}` — keep an existing contest subfolder if one exists. Do not drop a lone `.cpp` at the judge root. Do not migrate old flat files unless asked.

- `statement.md`: full restatement in our own words (I/O, constraints, samples, special cases). Not a summary. Not a wholesale paste.
- `solution.md`: forks, algorithm, tiny example, key insight, non-trivial code notes only. Named theory/algorithm/data structure gets a quick explanation (what it is, why it fits). Keep a visual here if it made the idea click. Keep the whole path when a submission is not AC (WA/TLE/…): what was tried, what the judge said, what that taught, and how the next fork fixed it — not only the final accepted write-up. After AC, any optimization worth seeing. Do not rewrite the AC'd code unless asked.
- `<PROBLEM_ID>.cpp`: clear code; do not mimic old `scanf`/golf style; comment only the *why* of non-trivial bits.

## Session loop

1. Create the folder and write `statement.md`. One problem per branch (`<judge>/<PROBLEM_ID>`, e.g. `spoj/LCS0`). Never a second problem on that branch.
2. Restate in chat (constraints included). Ask Ronald for his approach. Do not dump forks or the solution path first.
3. Guide his thinking toward the process that solves it: questions, the gap, the next fork — starting from what he said. If you name a theory, algorithm, or data structure, add a quick explanation — what it is and why it fits, not a textbook dump.
4. Prove the algorithm on a tiny example before code. If a picture would make it easier (time line, DP table, graph, shrinking range), draw it — Mermaid, compact ASCII, or a small table. Persist the useful ones in `solution.md`. No decoration.
5. Write `solution.md` as thinking settles. Code only after Ronald says go.
6. Never submit to the judge. Wait for Ronald to submit, then ask for the verdict (AC/WA/TLE, plus time and memory if he has them — optional). If not AC, debug the idea before the implementation. Update both files if the idea changes. Leave the failed attempt in `solution.md` as its own section (what we tried, the verdict, why it failed, the next fork) so the accepted path is a story, not a clean rewrite that erases the miss.
7. Close with the insight in `solution.md`.
8. After AC: optimization insights in `solution.md` (time, memory, or anything else that would actually improve it). Do not rewrite the accepted code unless asked.
9. When AC and post-AC are both in: squash-merge the branch into `main`, push `main`, delete the problem branch (local and remote if it exists). No pull requests.

## Hard nos

- Dump a finished solution before walking the thinking.
- Present the full solution path before Ronald has given an approach.
- Skip “why this, why not that.”
- Narrate obvious code (`read n`, plain loop bounds).
- Skip a visual when a time line, table, or graph would make the idea easier.
- Name a theory, algorithm, or data structure without a quick explanation of what it is and why it fits.
- Submit to an online judge, or assume AC without asking Ronald for the result.
- A second problem on the same branch, a pull request, or leaving a finished problem branch around.
